Yes, a mobile phone number's location data—such as cell tower triangulation or GPS coordinates—exclusively resides within the network provider’s operational infrastructure, including:  
(1) **Base station logs** (e.g., cell ID, signal strength);  
(2) **Core network databases** (e.g., MSC, HLR, EIR);  
(3) **Location services** (e.g., A-GPS, Wi-Fi positioning);  
(4) **Subscriber data records** (e.g., call detail records, CDRs).  

This data is stored on carrier servers and accessed only via authorized channels (e.g., law enforcement with a warrant). Third parties, including apps or device manufacturers, cannot access real-time location without user consent or legal authorization. The data is protected under privacy laws (e.g., GDPR, FCRA) and subject to strict access controls. Unauthorized access violates data protection and telecom regulations.

Under standard operational conditions, only the following entities can access a phone number’s real-time location data:  
(1) **Law enforcement agencies** with a valid court order or warrant;  
(2) **Emergency services** (e.g., 911, 112) during emergency calls;  
(3) **The user themselves**, via built-in location services (e.g., Find My iPhone, Android Location);  
(4) **Authorized third parties with explicit user consent** (e.g., family locator apps, ride-sharing services).  

No other entity—such as app developers, advertisers, or data brokers—can access real-time location data without consent or legal authority. Access is strictly controlled by network providers under privacy laws (e.g., GDPR, FCRA). Unauthorized access constitutes a breach of data protection and cybercrime laws. Location data remains protected and confined to the carrier’s infrastructure unless legally or consented to be shared.

