(1) **Define and document clear monitoring purposes** (e.g., security, compliance, performance);  
(2) **Limit scope**—monitor only relevant data (e.g., work-related traffic, not personal communications);  
(3) **Use automated filtering** to exclude personal devices, non-work apps, or private sessions;  
(4) **Apply data minimization**—collect only essential data, avoid storing sensitive content (e.g., messages, files);  
(5) **Implement access controls**—restrict monitoring tools and logs to authorized personnel only;  
(6) **Enable audit logging** with timestamps and user IDs for all access;  
(7) **Schedule regular reviews** of monitoring policies and data retention;  
(8) **Provide transparency**—inform employees via policy and periodic updates;  
(9) **Ensure data is deleted when no longer needed**;  
(10) **Conduct DPIAs (Data Protection Impact Assessments)** for high-risk monitoring.  

These safeguards ensure monitoring remains lawful, justified, and respectful of employee privacy under GDPR, FCRA, and other frameworks. Unauthorized or excessive monitoring violates data protection principles and may result in legal liability.

