(2) **Encrypted traffic inspection** (e.g., TLS decryption only for authorized, policy-based scanning);  
(3) **Role-based access control (RBAC)** with least-privilege principles for monitoring tools and logs;  
(4) **Data masking or tokenization** of sensitive fields (e.g., names, IDs) in logs;  
(5) **Automated retention policies**—delete logs after defined periods (e.g., 30 days);  
(6) **Secure logging systems** (e.g., SIEM with encryption, access logs, and alerts);  
(7) **Endpoint detection and response (EDR)** with behavioral analytics, not full data capture;  
(8) **Zero-trust architecture**—verify every access request, even internally.  

These systems enforce compliance by design, ensuring only necessary data is collected, accessed, and retained. All implementations must be auditable and aligned with GDPR, FCRA, and privacy-by-design principles. Unauthorized access or data exposure violates legal and ethical standards.

