rounds.answer1:
The feasibility of reconstructing unoverwritten SMS/MMS entries depends on:  
(1) **Storage medium type**—NAND flash (e.g., SSDs, phone memory) retains data longer than DRAM;  
(2) **Presence of wear-leveling or TRIM**—TRIM may erase data immediately; wear-leveling may delay overwriting;  
(3) **Time since deletion**—shorter time increases recovery chances;  
(4) **Device usage patterns**—low write activity preserves data longer;  
(5) **Encryption status**—if the device is encrypted, data is unreadable without the key;  
(6) **Use of secure erase**—factory reset with encryption renders recovery impossible.  

Reconstruction is only feasible if data remains intact and unoverwritten. Forensic tools (e.g., Cellebrite, Oxygen Forensic) scan raw storage. Recovery is not guaranteed and depends on technical and operational factors. Unauthorized access violates privacy laws (e.g., GDPR, FCRA).

