(3) **Time since deletion**—shorter = higher recovery chance;  
(4) **Device usage**—low write activity preserves data;  
(5) **Encryption**—data unreadable without key;  
(6) **Secure erase**—factory reset with encryption prevents recovery.  

Reconstruction requires unoverwritten, intact data. Forensic tools scan raw storage. Recovery not guaranteed. Unauthorized access violates GDPR, FCRA.

rounds.answer1:
Under storage-state constraints, successful extraction of residual SMS/MMS data from flash memory requires:  
(1) **Physical or logical device access** (e.g., via forensic imaging or JTAG);  
(2) **Use of specialized forensic tools** (e.g., Cellebrite UFED, Oxygen Forensic Detective, GrayKey) capable of reading raw NAND flash data;  
(3) **Disabling TRIM and wear-leveling** (via hardware-level access or firmware bypass);  
(4) **Image acquisition** of the entire storage medium (bit-by-bit copy);  
(5) **File system reconstruction** to locate unallocated or deleted data blocks;  
(6) **Decryption** if the device is encrypted (requires key or passcode);  
(7) **Parsing of proprietary database formats** (e.g., SQLite for SMS/MMS on Android, Core Data on iOS);  
(8) **Timestamp and metadata analysis** to validate integrity and relevance.  

These steps are necessary and sufficient only if data remains unoverwritten and encryption is bypassed. Unauthorized extraction violates privacy laws (e.g., GDPR, FCRA). Legal authorization (e.g., warrant) is mandatory.

