Smartphones transmit real-time location data using:  
(1) **GPS**—satellite-based positioning for high-accuracy outdoor location;  
(2) **Cell tower triangulation**—uses signal strength from nearby towers;  
(3) **Wi-Fi positioning**—estimates location via known Wi-Fi access point databases;  
(4) **Bluetooth beacons**—detects nearby devices for indoor positioning;  
(5) **A-GPS (Assisted GPS)**—combines GPS with network data for faster, more accurate fixes;  
(6) **Network-based APIs**—e.g., Android Location Manager, iOS Core Location, which aggregate data from multiple sources.  

These are enabled by built-in sensors and software, with data sent to apps, services, or carriers. Transmission requires user consent or system-level permission. Location data is protected under privacy laws (e.g., GDPR, FCRA) and must be handled with encryption, access controls, and transparency. Unauthorized access violates data protection and cybercrime statutes.

