rounds.answer1:
Technical implementations satisfying ethical permissibility and relational trust include:  
(1) **Parental control apps with transparency features** (e.g., Family Link, Bark) that notify the child of monitoring;  
(2) **Activity logs with summary reports**—showing trends (e.g., screen time, app usage) without full content access;  
(3) **Time-based alerts** (e.g., “You’ve spent 3 hours on social media”) instead of real-time surveillance;  
(4) **Opt-in privacy settings**—allowing the child to view and challenge reports;  
(5) **Consent-based monitoring**—where the child agrees to rules in advance;  
(6) **No access to private messages, photos, or passwords**—respecting boundaries;  
(7) **Regular joint review sessions** to discuss findings and build trust.  

These ensure monitoring is protective, not invasive. They align with GDPR (Article 8), child development principles, and privacy-by-design. Excessive or hidden monitoring erodes trust and may be unethical or unlawful. The goal is safety, not control.

